ORGO.reg({"types":{"zaitsev-hofmann":{"id":"zaitsev-hofmann","title":"Saytzeff or Hofmann: which alkene is major","exam":"Lecture 11/12","asks":"An elimination can give two alkenes. Say which is major and how the leaving group or the base changes the answer.","know":[{"h":"<b>Saytzeff product</b> (also spelled Zaitsev): the <b>more substituted</b> alkene. <b>Hofmann product</b>: the <b>less substituted</b> alkene. Substitution is counted as the number of carbons attached to the two C=C carbons.","from":"Lecture 11/12, p. 4"},{"h":"<b>The lecture data</b>: 2-hexyl-LG with {{NaOCH3}} in {{CH3OH}} gives 2-hexene (Saytzeff) and 1-hexene (Hofmann). I: 81% and 19%. Br: 72% and 28%. Cl: 67% and 33%. F: 30% and 70%.","from":"Lecture 11/12, p. 4"},{"h":"<b>The trend</b>: the better the leaving group, the more Saytzeff product. I > Br > Cl all give mostly 2-hexene. Only fluoride gives mostly 1-hexene.","from":"Lecture 11/12, p. 4"},{"h":"<b>Fluoride is a \"Hofmann leaving group\".</b> {{F-}} is a poor leaving group (HF has {{pKa}} 4, against −8 to −10 for HCl, HBr and HI), so the C-H bond breaks ahead of the C-F bond. The lecture calls this an {{E1cb}}-like {{E2}}.","from":"Lecture 11/12, p. 4; 10/29, p. 1"},{"h":"<b>Why that gives Hofmann.</b> A transition state in which C-H breaks first has negative charge (carbanion character) on the carbon losing the hydrogen. A carbanion is better on the less substituted carbon, so the end {{CH3}} loses its hydrogen.","from":"Lecture 11/12, p. 4"},{"h":"<b>Why Saytzeff is the normal result.</b> With a good leaving group the transition state already looks like the alkene, and a more substituted alkene is more stable. The more stable transition state forms faster.","from":"Lecture 11/12, p. 4; standard"},{"h":"<b>A bulky base favours Hofmann.</b> tert-Butoxide, {{(CH3)3CO-}}, is large. It reaches the exposed hydrogens of an end {{CH3}} more easily than a hydrogen on a crowded inner carbon.","from":"Lecture 11/05, p. 3; flow chart; standard"},{"h":"<b>Small strong bases</b> ({{CH3O-}}, {{CH3CH2O-}}, {{HO-}}) with Cl, Br, I or {{OTs}} give Saytzeff.","from":"Lecture 11/12, p. 4"},{"h":"<b>Anti-periplanar comes first.</b> The hydrogen must be able to stand anti to the leaving group. The lecture calls this the most important factor for {{E2}}. If the Saytzeff hydrogen cannot get anti (as on some cyclohexanes), the other alkene forms whatever the base or leaving group.","from":"Lecture 11/12, p. 3; FA24 Exam 2, Q3D"},{"h":"<b>Anti-periplanar also fixes E or Z</b> when the carbon losing the hydrogen has only one: (2S,3S)-2-bromo-3-methylpentane gives (E)-3-methyl-2-pentene as its major {{E2}} product.","from":"Lecture 11/12, p. 2 (homework)"},{"h":"<b>2-Hexene is two compounds</b>, E and Z. The lecture's Saytzeff percentage is the two together. E is the larger share because its alkyl groups are farther apart.","from":"Lecture 11/12, p. 4; standard"},{"h":"<b>Skill: classify each possible alkene</b> as monosubstituted, disubstituted, trisubstituted or tetrasubstituted before deciding.","from":"standard"}],"method":["Find the carbon with the leaving group and the carbons next to it that have hydrogens.","Draw the alkene from each and count the carbons attached to each C=C: the more substituted one is Saytzeff, the less substituted one is Hofmann.","Check geometry: can each hydrogen stand anti-periplanar to the leaving group? On a ring or with fixed stereocentres, an alkene whose hydrogen cannot is ruled out.","Look at the leaving group. I, Br, Cl or OTs: Saytzeff. F: Hofmann.","Look at the base. Small (methoxide, ethoxide, hydroxide): Saytzeff. Bulky (tert-butoxide): Hofmann.","Give the major alkene and, when the question is about 2-hexyl compounds, the lecture's percentage."],"pitfalls":[{"h":"Mixing up the names: Saytzeff is the more substituted alkene, Hofmann the less substituted one."},{"h":"Treating fluoride like the other halides. With F the major product flips to Hofmann (70% 1-hexene)."},{"h":"Forgetting that tert-butoxide is bulky and writing the Saytzeff alkene as major."},{"h":"Applying the Saytzeff rule before checking that the hydrogen can be anti-periplanar to the leaving group."},{"h":"Calling a result Hofmann or Saytzeff when the halide has only one kind of beta hydrogen and can give only one alkene."}],"links":{"read":["e2","choose"]}}},"items":{},"mols":{}});
